The server task and the client task both print, and nothing orders them against
each other. `serveDefault(..., 2)` returns once it has ACCEPTED two connections
and prints `server done served=2` from the server task, while the client task is
still awaiting the second response - so that line may land either between the two
`status=` lines or after both.

Measured rather than assumed: 60 unloaded runs all produced one order, and 60
runs with eight busy cores alongside produced the other order 5 times. An
expectation pinning either one is a test that fails a few percent of the time on
a loaded CI machine.

The socket-free halves of the same behaviour are asserted by http_no_socket,
http_parse_smoke and http_url_smoke, which have no second thread to race.
